  "description": "A parametric COVE LIGHT on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A SEGMENT — a wall. This is the DETAIL, not the tape: the board, the lip that hides the tape and the gap the light gets to open out across. `led-strip` is the tape on its own, for when the detail already exists in the model.\n\nTHE TAPE IS SET BACK FROM THE LIP, NOT FROM THE WALL. The lip is what conceals it, so the concealment is a distance between those two and has nothing to do with where the wall is. Move the wall and nothing about the concealment changes — which is correct, and would not be true of a setback measured the other way.\n\nTHE CUT-OFF ANGLE IS THE NUMBER TO LOOK AT. Sight the top of the lip against the near edge of the tape: below that angle the source is hidden, above it you are looking straight at a line of diodes. A 30 mm lip with the tape hard against it conceals nothing, and no amount of lumens fixes that.\n\nWASH HEIGHT IS THE SECOND NUMBER. The light has to open out across the gap between the tape and the slab. Under about 150 mm a cove reads as a bright stripe rather than a lit ceiling, so `washHeight` and `slotClear` are both reported rather than left for the eye.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: a 3 m run, 180 mm deep, board 250 mm below a 2.9 m slab, 90 mm lip, tape 40 mm behind it — a 65.8° cut-off and 246 mm of wash.\n\nWHAT THIS CANNOT DO. The cove is straight. A curved or stepped cove is several of these. The ceiling luminance is lumens over the wash area and is a sanity figure, not a simulation.",
